---
// /trust — what Odal can and cannot see, stated as properties of the software.
//
// Describes the self-hosted node only. Managed deployments are an option under
// consideration and are mentioned on the roadmap and nowhere else, so this page
// makes no claim about a deployment that does not exist.
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Section from "../components/Section.astro";

const title = "Trust | Odal Node";
const description =
  "What Odal can and cannot see, and why that does not depend on trusting us.";

const guarantees = [
  {
    title: "Every field carries your signature",
    body: "Each published passport is signed with your key, so a change made after signing fails verification. Anyone can check that without us.",
  },
  {
    title: "We cannot reach your node",
    body: "When you run the node, we are not part of the deployment and have no access to anything it stores.",
  },
  {
    title: "Nobody signs for you",
    body: "Your signing key is generated and held on your own infrastructure. We cannot sign on your behalf.",
  },
];
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <Section h1 eyebrow="Data and trust" title="What Odal Node can and cannot see." wide>
    <p>
      Odal Node is built on a proof-bound architecture: the manufacturer's
      product data is validated, signed and stored on infrastructure the
      manufacturer controls, and every published field carries the
      manufacturer's signature. This is how the software works, whoever
      operates the node.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section eyebrow="Guarantees" title="Three things that do not depend on trusting us." tone="tinted" wide>
    <ul class="not-prose m-0 grid list-none gap-6 p-0 md:grid-cols-3">
      {guarantees.map((g) => (
        <li class="card m-0 p-6">
          <h3 class="m-0 text-base font-semibold text-neutral-900">{g.title}</h3>
          <p class="m-0 mt-2 text-sm leading-body text-neutral-600">{g.body}</p>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </Section>

  <Section eyebrow="Scan telemetry" title="What a scan count is, and what it is not." wide>
    <p>
      When a passport is resolved, the node can record that it happened, as a
      count per passport per day, and nothing more. A scan count records{" "}
      <strong>nothing about the person who scanned</strong>: no IP address,
      device, location, identity or session. The software has no field to store
      any of it, so none of it can be kept or leaked. Generating a QR-code image
      is counted separately, because it measures label printing, not readers.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section eyebrow="How it works" title="How it works, step by step." tone="muted" wide>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Import.</strong> Product data arrives at your node as CSV, Excel
        or an ERP export. This happens on infrastructure you control.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Validate.</strong> The node checks the data locally against the
        product group's rules. Nothing is sent to a third party to be checked.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Sign.</strong> Your private signing key, generated and held on
        your own infrastructure, signs the validated passport. The signature is
        tied to an identity on a web domain you control.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Publish.</strong> The signed passport's public part becomes
        readable through its QR code and GS1 Digital Link; other parts are
        shown only to readers with a credential.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Verify.</strong> Any consumer, authority or recycler checks the
        signature against the public identity published on your web domain.
        Verification does not need Odal to be online, or even to exist.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </Section>
</Base>
